# The distribution header reference from plan/00-foundation/01-vision-and-scope.md.
#
# Three circuits: one source in the shared header and two pumped consumers. Every consumer runs cooler
# than the 60 C supply, so each blends supply water with its own return.
#
# `HS1` states `power` and `out` and not `in`, as `01` says it must: `out=60` is the one absolute
# temperature a closed circuit needs (`D-90`) -- it pays for the energy balance dropped as the level
# and pins nothing else -- and the return header's 30 C is an answer, not a demand (`F-16`). Omit the
# power and the two loads size it to +54 kW from the closed-circuit balance; both spellings solve.
# The AHU and radiator pumps overcome the pressure drops in their complete circuits, including the
# shared source path; there is no pump in the header (`S-55` is what the analysis still gets wrong
# about that shape when a source valve is added). Solved, it reaches `01`'s figures: 0.2871 and
# 0.3589 kg/s round the coils, 0.1914 and 0.2392 kg/s drawn from the header, 0.4306 kg/s through
# HS1, the valves at 0.63 and 0.62 of travel. `S-58` is why it did not until 2026-09-14: a junction
# mixed its inlets by a plain average, so no valve position could move a mixed temperature.
#
# `S-39`/`D-86`: the pressure datum sits on `N1`, the return header's junction, which is where an
# engineer would put it -- the expansion vessel connection. It admits no mass: a `p=` on an interior
# node is a *datum*, and only a `supply` or a `return` is a boundary. Until `D-86` it was read as a
# boundary, which turned this closed circuit into an open one, dropped no redundant mass balance, and
# left the system deficient by exactly one -- fixable only by moving the datum somewhere unnatural.
#
# `F-16`/`F-17`: each consumer's recirculation leg is connected, so `in=50` on a 60 C header is a
# demand its valve can actually meet. Both ends of each attachment are written out because
# `supply`/`return` cannot express a mixing subcircuit: the rule takes the first unconnected inlet,
# and on this shape that is the valve's outlet, which wires the consumer's return to the *supply*
# header and leaves every header branch carrying zero.
#
# `S-41`: the datum being on `N1` was not enough on its own. `D-86` fixed the three places that decide
# whether mass crosses a boundary and missed `IsClosed`, so this circuit still read as *open* -- with
# zero boundaries -- and no energy balance was dropped as its level. A closed circuit's energy balances
# are one short of independent, because adding the same enthalpy to every node satisfies all of them.
#
# `D-91`: a `load` states positive capacity and lowering supplies the negative side-1 heat flow. `dt`
# remains a positive magnitude. The neutral `heat_exchanger` spelling remains explicitly signed.
#
# `C-66`: the tap branches carry real pipework. A branch written as a bare node-to-node connection
# drops 0 kPa, and a valve sized against no resistance at all sits inside the solver's smoothing band,
# so the rule declines rather than inventing an answer.

fluidscript 1
project static plant_01

circuit heating 100
fluid water

HS1     heat_exchanger power=54 kW out=60          # the plant-side source; the outlet is the level

connections
N1 - HS1 - N3                                     # return is heated directly into the supply header
N3 - N4                                           # supply header
N6 - N5
N5 - N1                                           # return header

N1 node p=250

circuit AHU 101

HE_AHU  load in=50 out=30 power=24 kW
TV_AHU  three_way_valve
PU_AHU  pump
PA1     pipe length=12 dn=25
PA2     pipe length=12 dn=25

connections
N3 - PA1 - TV_AHU.a                               # supply-header water enters the hot port
NM_AHU - TV_AHU.b                                 # 30 C coil return recirculates to the cold port
TV_AHU.ab - PU_AHU - HE_AHU - NM_AHU              # pump follows the valve and drives the whole loop
NM_AHU - PA2 - N5                                 # injected header flow returns to the plant

circuit radiators 102

HE_RAD  load in=50 out=30 power=30 kW
TV_RAD  three_way_valve
PU_RAD  pump
PR1     pipe length=18 dn=25
PR2     pipe length=18 dn=25

connections
N4 - PR1 - TV_RAD.a
NM_RAD - TV_RAD.b
TV_RAD.ab - PU_RAD - HE_RAD - NM_RAD
NM_RAD - PR2 - N6
